{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1852178230/11/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"221dfab4\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nPropagate a repeating striped pattern from a yellow border segment toward the opposite edge, recoloring foreground objects on certain stripes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Foreground blobs of another color scattered in the interior.\n- A short yellow (4) segment sits on one border edge.\n\nRule:\n- From the yellow segment, march toward the opposite edge in steps of 2 cells, cycling colors Y, Y, G (4, 4, 3). The 1-cell gap between stripes is forced to bg.\n- Yellow stripes paint only within the segment's span.\n- Green stripes additionally recolor all non-bg pixels across the entire row/column to green.\n\nKey insight: Green stripes act as a \"flood\" that recolors existing objects on the full row/column, while yellow stripes are confined to the segment span.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2b83f449\" lines=\"10-22\">\n<description>\nSimulate tokens falling through a pipe network after gates rotate open.\n\nInput structure:\n- Wall (0), bg (pipe fill), gate (horizontal 1×3 bars), token (small edge pixels).\n\nRule:\n- Rotate each gate into a vertical blocker; gate cells become bg.\n- Tokens drop downward through bg, choosing the extreme column for their side (left→min, right→max).\n- Tokens settle at true pipe ends (adjacent to wall/boundary), packing inward.\n\nKey insight: Tokens select drop columns by entry side and land at wall-bounded endpoints, ignoring blocker-bounded ones.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1be83260\" lines=\"41-58\">\n<description>\nDecode the colored slot map inside the marked motif and rebuild the motif lattice with those colors.\n\nInput structure:\n- A blank background surrounds a rectangular lattice of identical motifs.\n- The motif rows and columns are separated by one background line.\n- One motif has colored marker pixels in its internal odd-row/odd-column slots.\n\nRule:\n- The motif size is 2R+1 by 2C+1, where R by C is the motif lattice size.\n- Read the marked motif's odd internal slots as the color map for the R by C output motifs.\n- Slots that still contain the repeated motif color map to that repeated motif color.\n- Rebuild the compact lattice on a canvas filled with the repeated motif color,\n  painting each motif mask with its decoded color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The holes and filled odd slots inside one motif are a miniature legend for recoloring the entire repeated motif lattice.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"dce56571\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nStraighten the single foreground mask into a centered horizontal segment of the same color and length.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- All non-background pixels share one foreground color.\n- The number of foreground pixels fits in one centered row of the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Erase the original foreground mask.\n- Count its pixels, keep its color, and paint that many pixels as a horizontal segment.\n- Place the segment on the middle row with equal background margin on the left and right.\n\nKey insight:\n- The original foreground shape is irrelevant except for its color and area; the output is the area normalized into the grid's central horizontal line.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2601afb7\" lines=\"30-45\">\n<description>\nRotate each vertical bar's color one slot right and its height one slot left.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background surrounds several monochrome one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Every bar is bottom-aligned and occupies a distinct column.\n- The ordered bar slots are read from left to right.\n\nRule\n- Keep the grid shape and the bar columns unchanged.\n- At slot i, use the color from the previous slot cyclically.\n- At slot i, use the height from the next slot cyclically.\n\nKey insight\n- Color and height are independent cyclic attributes of the ordered bar slots:\n  colors rotate right while heights rotate left.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"0934a4d8\" lines=\"75-90\">\n<description>\nReveal the rectangular patch hidden by the solid marker using the grid's reflection symmetries.\n\nInput structure:\n- A square colored pattern with one solid rectangular marker color occluding a patch.\n- The visible pattern has horizontal and vertical reflection symmetry on a large lower/right strip.\n- The top and left outer strips also expose transpose counterparts for cells not recoverable by those reflections.\n\nRule:\n- Find the unique color whose full extent is a solid rectangle; this rectangle is the output region.\n- Infer the reflected strip start as the smallest row/column offset whose lower/right strips are mirror-consistent, ignoring marker pixels.\n- For each marker pixel, copy the unique non-marker value in its horizontal/vertical reflection orbit; if that orbit is entirely hidden in the outer strip, use the transposed top/left-strip counterpart.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker is not part of the pattern; it covers a missing rectangle whose cells are still determined by the visible mirror or transpose copies elsewhere in the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"78332cb0\" lines=\"10-23\">\n<description>\nReorder 5×5 blocks into a connected strip by matching edge foreground pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background; sep = divider color (full row/column lines).\n- Several 5×5 sprite blocks in a grid, separated by sep lines.\n\nRule:\n- Two blocks \"connect\" when their facing edges share foreground pixels at the same offset (e.g., A's right edge ∩ B's left edge).\n- Chain all blocks in the orientation (horizontal or vertical) that admits a complete sequence.\n- Output is a 1×N strip of blocks in chain order, separated by sep lines.\n\nKey insight: Edge-contact overlap determines both block ordering and output orientation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"833966f4\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nSwap the upper two-pixel segment and the lower two-pixel segment around the fixed center pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a 5x1 vertical column of individual colored pixels.\n- The middle pixel is a separator between two same-size two-pixel segments.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the middle pixel in place.\n- Reverse the order of the two pixels above the middle.\n- Reverse the order of the two pixels below the middle.\n\nKey insight:\n- The transformation is local vertical order reversal on each side of the center, not a color-specific rule.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d93c6891\" lines=\"84-101\">\n<description>\nMove each gray bar into its adjacent orange rectangle as full stripes from the contacted edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The scene uses black outside space, yellow fill, gray one-cell-thick bars, and solid orange rectangles.\n- Each gray bar 4-touches exactly one orange rectangle; some orange rectangles have no touching gray bar.\n- Gray bars touching the same orange rectangle all have the same orientation.\n\nRule\n- Recolor every original gray cell to yellow.\n- For each orange rectangle touched by gray bars, count the gray cells assigned to that rectangle.\n- Horizontal gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange rows starting from the contacted top or bottom row.\n- Vertical gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange columns starting from the contacted left or right column.\n- Orange rectangles with no touching gray bar remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The gray pixels are not copied by shape; their count is transferred into the neighboring orange rectangle as edge-aligned row or column stripes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a09f6c25\" lines=\"33-50\">\n<description>\nRecolor each non-singleton red object by its symmetry axis and erase isolated red pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color and red 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Every non-singleton red object has exactly one reflection symmetry category:\n  horizontal, vertical, or diagonal.\n- Single red pixels are noise.\n\nRule:\n- Start from an all-background output of the same shape.\n- Recolor horizontally symmetric red objects blue, vertically symmetric objects\n  green, and diagonally symmetric objects pink.\n- Leave isolated red pixels as background.\n\nKey insight: The target color is not tied to object position or size; it is a\nlabel for the object's reflection symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"eee78d87\" lines=\"40-55\">\n<description>\nDecode the colored 3x3 contour motif into a 16x16 periodic lattice with the motif stamped in maroon at the center.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is 6x6 with orange background and one non-orange color.\n- The non-orange pixels have a tight 3x3 bbox containing one contour glyph.\n- The glyph is one of the task's period-3 contour forms: plus, H, X, or four corners.\n\nRule:\n- Decode the 3x3 glyph into the period-3 orange phase tile.\n- Fill a black 16x16 canvas by repeating that orange phase tile.\n- Tile the original 3x3 glyph 2x2 and recolor its true pixels to maroon in the centered 6x6 region.\n\nKey insight:\n- The colored object is not copied at its input location; it is a small contour code whose phase tells where orange appears in the repeated lattice.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"cf5fd0ad\" lines=\"10-22\">\n<description>\nBuild a four-quadrant rotational tiling of the input square.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
